<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nationalism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as nationalist</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nationalism is the political idea that a nation, defined by shared language, culture or history, should possess its own state or self-government. From the nineteenth century it undermined multinational empires. It drove demands from the many nationalities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a>, fuelled independence movements against the Ottoman and French empires, and shaped Chinese responses to the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Scholars frequently place the beginning of nationalism in the late 18th or early 19th century, with the American Declaration of Independence or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, though there is ongoing debate about forms of it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and even antiquity. The French Revolution of 1789 is seen as an important starting point, above all for its impact on Germans, Italians and European intellectuals. The template of mobilising public opinion around a new state based on popular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> went back further, to earlier constitutionalist movements such as the Corsican Republic and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a>.</p><p>Primordialism, which arose in the Romantic era, held that there have always been nations, and most scholars have rejected it. Modernist accounts argue that nationalism arises in societies with a self-sustaining industrial economy, a central authority and a shared language. Benedict Anderson tied it to print <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a> and printed media in vernacular languages, and described nations as socially constructed communities imagined by those who belong to them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Nationalism holds that the nation should be congruent with the state, that it should govern itself free from outside interference, and that it is the only rightful source of political power. It aims to build a single national identity from shared characteristics such as culture, ethnicity, homeland, language, religion or a belief in a shared history.</p><p>The most common classification is civic or ethnic, a distinction popularised in the 1950s by Hans Kohn and criticised since the 1980s as too rigid. Civic nationalism defines the nation as an association of people with equal political rights; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-renan">Ernest Renan</a> in 1882 called the nation a daily referendum. Ethnic nationalism defines it by shared heritage, language, faith and ancestry. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> nationalism arose among the descendants of colonisers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> seized control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and Portugal, and independence was won in civil wars between 1808 and 1826.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In the 19th century nationalism became one of the most significant political and social forces in history, and it is typically listed among the top causes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. Napoleon's conquests of the German and Italian states around 1800 to 1806 stimulated demands for national unity. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1905 spurred new interest in nationalism in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, and in China Sun Yat-sen founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> in defiance of the declining empire. Nationalism has been a feature of movements for freedom and justice, and has also been used to legitimise racial, ethnic and religious divisions and to start wars.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nationalism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Nationalism</b>, drawing on Rousseau’s Social Contract, aimed to make a nation state from the remnants of the multi-ethnic Ottoman Empire, defining the Turkish nation as the people who established the Republic and promoting the motto “How happy is the one who says I am a Turk”; it renounced…</blockquote><a href="/ideology/kemalism">Kemalism · Core Ideas</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Nationalism</b> grew after the Cavite mutiny of 1872, and the execution of Jose Rizal in 1896 fed the Philippine Revolution.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-general-of-the-philippines">Captaincy General of the Philippines</a></li></ul></section>
